      "18700": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "time": "2024-04-09T10:02:10Z",
            "attachment_id": 3328,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "creator": "ikrylov",
            "id": 100711,
            "is_private": false,
            "tags": [],
            "text": "Created attachment 3328\nSuggested changes\n\nI would like to suggest two changes to the way `parallel` behaves around errors.\n\nFirst, I would like `parallel` to use out-of-band information, i.e. `recvData(node)$success`, as a test for whether evaluation has succeeded instead of using `inherits(value, 'try-error')`. This test has served us well, but it's not perfect. Imagine a situation where someone would like to use `clusterMap()` for a long computing job. The code of the job is flaky, so the expression is wrapped into `try()` as a precaution. Later, when the error happens, it is handled by `try`, but the whole result is still discarded despite `$success` was true.\n\nThe proposed change complicates the users of `recvResult` and `recvOneResult`.\n\nSecond, I would like `staticClusterApply` and `dynamicClusterApply` to avoid sending more jobs to the cluster once it becomes evident that the whole run is doomed.\n\nFor `staticClusterApply`, this means moving `checkForRemoteErrors` inside the per-node loop. For `dynamicClusterApply`, this means introducing a new function to \"drain\" the outstanding results once an operation fails and then raise the error.\n\nPlease let me know if there is anything I should improve about the patch. In particular, I'm not happy about the test for early stop on error.",
            "bug_id": 18700,
            "count": 0,
            "creation_time": "2024-04-09T10:02:10Z"
          },
          {
            "creation_time": "2024-04-23T12:46:56Z",
            "creator": "ikrylov",
            "is_markdown": true,
            "count": 1,
            "bug_id": 18700,
            "text": "Created attachment 3341\nAdjusted patch\n\nApologies for not correctly \"draining\" the cluster in case when the number of remaining jobs was less than `min(n, p) - 1`. This has now been fixed.",
